+      "severity": "MEDIUM",
+      "claim": "Darwin’s On the Origin of Species (1859) had suggested that humans evolved from animals",
+      "truth": "On the Origin of Species set out evolution by natural selection but did not present a human-evolution argument; Darwin addressed human evolution directly in The Descent of Man (1871).",
+      "evidence": "Primary text context; Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1859) and The Descent of Man (1871).",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F2",
+      "lesson": 1,
+      "field": "practice_questions",
+      "severity": "HIGH",
+      "claim": "40 marks — Shakespeare Essay",
+      "truth": "This is the 19th-century prose question, not Shakespeare. Only extract-based tasks should be presented as the 40-mark exam format; discursive prompts are useful only as untimed whole-text practice.",
+      "evidence": "Specification sections 2a and 3a; supplied board facts for Component 01 Section B.",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F3",
+      "lesson": 2,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "MEDIUM",
+      "claim": "Bye Street",
+      "truth": "The text calls the location a ‘by-street’; it is not a proper name.",
+      "evidence": "Primary text ch. 1, lines 195 and 245; ch. 2, line 813.",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F4",
+      "lesson": 2,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "HIGH",
+      "claim": "‘small man’; ‘a look so ugly it brought out the sweat on me like running’; Carew’s ‘silvery hair’; ‘trampled’ in the Carew scene",
+      "truth": "Enfield says ‘little man’ and that Hyde ‘gave me one look, so ugly that it brought out the sweat on me like running’. Carew has ‘white hair’, and Hyde is ‘trampling’ him after clubbing him to the earth.",
+      "evidence": "Primary text ch. 1, lines 281–303; ch. 4, lines 1383 and 1421–1427.",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F5",
+      "lesson": 2,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "MEDIUM",
+      "claim": "The novella’s first moment of clear violence comes with Sir Danvers Carew’s murder.",
+      "truth": "Hyde’s trampling of the child in Chapter 1 is already a clear act of violence; Carew’s murder is an escalation.",
+      "evidence": "Primary text ch. 1, lines 281–303; ch. 4, lines 1413–1427.",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F6",
+      "lesson": 2,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "HIGH",
+      "claim": "Enfield tells Utterson, who reads the will, who hears Poole, who hears the maid.",
+      "truth": "An anonymous third-person narrator follows Utterson. Enfield’s account and the maid’s report are embedded accounts; Poole does not hear the maid’s report.",
+      "evidence": "Primary text chs. 1–4, especially ch. 4 line 1483, where the inspector narrates what the maid saw.",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F7",
+      "lesson": 2,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "LOW",
+      "claim": "a small red shilling edition",
+      "truth": "The first edition was a paper-covered volume costing one shilling; ‘red’ is unsupported.",
+      "evidence": "Project Gutenberg, The Works of Robert Louis Stevenson, Swanston Edition, vol. I, publication account.",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F8",
+      "lesson": 2,
+      "field": "practice_questions",
+      "severity": "HIGH",
+      "claim": "40 marks — Shakespeare Essay",
