+      "lesson": 2,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "HIGH",
+      "claim": "‘small man’; ‘a look so ugly it brought out the sweat on me like running’; Carew’s ‘silvery hair’; ‘trampled’ in the Carew scene",
+      "truth": "Enfield says ‘little man’ and that Hyde ‘gave me one look, so ugly that it brought out the sweat on me like running’. Carew has ‘white hair’, and Hyde is ‘trampling’ him after clubbing him to the earth.",
+      "evidence": "Primary text ch. 1, lines 281–303; ch. 4, lines 1383 and 1421–1427.",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F5",
+      "lesson": 2,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "MEDIUM",
+      "claim": "The novella’s first moment of clear violence comes with Sir Danvers Carew’s murder.",
+      "truth": "Hyde’s trampling of the child in Chapter 1 is already a clear act of violence; Carew’s murder is an escalation.",
+      "evidence": "Primary text ch. 1, lines 281–303; ch. 4, lines 1413–1427.",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F6",
+      "lesson": 2,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "HIGH",
+      "claim": "Enfield tells Utterson, who reads the will, who hears Poole, who hears the maid.",
+      "truth": "An anonymous third-person narrator follows Utterson. Enfield’s account and the maid’s report are embedded accounts; Poole does not hear the maid’s report.",
+      "evidence": "Primary text chs. 1–4, especially ch. 4 line 1483, where the inspector narrates what the maid saw.",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
