+      "truth": "Jekyll calls Lanyon a ‘hide-bound pedant’; ‘the practical side of medicine’ is not in the text.",
+      "evidence": "Primary text ch. 3, lines 1227–1233; no occurrence of ‘practical side’ in the primary text.",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F12",
+      "lesson": 3,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "MEDIUM",
+      "claim": "early Freud ... questioning the unity of the self [in the 1880s]",
+      "truth": "Freud’s psychoanalytic model belongs mainly to the 1890s and later. William James is an appropriate contemporary reference for 1880s debates about consciousness.",
+      "evidence": "Publication chronology: William James’s psychology work appeared through the 1870s–1880s; Freud’s psychoanalytic work developed in the 1890s.",
+      "verdict": "FIX"
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "F13",
+      "lesson": 3,
+      "field": "content_html",
+      "severity": "HIGH",
